# Cross-platform parity (ios + android)

This monorepo is intended to ship the same product on **iOS** (`ios/`) and **Android**
(`android/`). Android has not started yet; the directory exists so the port never requires moving
iOS files.

## Rule
A behavior, API, analytics, or user-visible UI change in one platform **requires the matching change
in the other**, or an explicit PR note explaining why parity is deferred.

Timing of the Android update is governed by `ios-first.mdc` (iOS first; port after iOS commit or
explicit ask). While `android/` is empty, every iOS change defers parity by definition — no note
needed until the port lands.

## Shared seams (keep names aligned)
LibraryService → PublicationOpener → ViewModel → UI + Coordinator + AnalyticsTimer + Errors
TTSService → Navigator decorations → reading position store

## Product contracts that must stay aligned
- Reading position is a Readium `Locator` serialized to JSON. The JSON is wire-compatible between
  the Swift and Kotlin toolkits — same keys (`href`, `type`, `locations`, `text`). Never invent a
  private position format.
- Position writes are throttled, not fired per page turn.
- TTS starts from the locator of the current text selection and continues across chapters until
  explicitly stopped.
- The spoken sentence is highlighted, and pages turn from the spoken word range.
- TTS and highlighting are offered for EPUB only. Both toolkits lack them for PDF; hide the controls
  rather than failing at runtime.
- Imported books are copied into app storage; the library structure lives in the database, not in
  the filesystem layout.
- Analytics events carry `duration_ms`: `import_book`, `opening_publication`, `tts_start_latency`.
- Accessibility ids use dotted `screen.element` names and must match across platforms
  (`.accessibilityIdentifier` on iOS, `Modifier.testTag` on Android).
